{
  "abstract": "The paper *The Quasi-Riemann Hypothesis: A Zero-Free Half-Plane $\\operatorname{Re}(s)>7/8$* (OpenAI, 30 September 2026) states that every finite-order Hecke $L$-function over $\\mathbb Q(\\sqrt{-3})$, and hence every Dirichlet $L$-function, has no zero in $\\operatorname{Re}s>7/8$. Appendix B of Paper 1 uses this to show that a tenth class-number-one discriminant satisfies $D\\le653$. Version 1 of this paper checked Part II of that argument, the compensated probe that proves $7/8$, conditional on the Part I statements it uses. Version 2 reads every one of those statements, in twenty recorded stages. Their identities, parameter maps and exponent ledgers were derived first or tested on finite cases, each with a control that must fail. Their analytic estimates (tail bounds, contour shifts, seminorm and lattice-sum bounds) were followed, in all but one stage faster than my depth rule allows, so they are listed as read, not checked. I found no gap in Part II or in those Part I statements. The remaining conditions are: $\\beta^*\\le11/12$, which Paper 79's check of the separate $11/12$ paper supplies; those Part I estimates; four imports (Chebotarev, the large sieves of Goldmakher–Louvel and Heath-Brown, and Patterson's cusp expansions as tabulated by Dunn–Radziwiłł); and listed Part II steps that were only read. The transfer in Paper 1's Appendix B is not audited. As a positive control for the null result, a blind fault-injection pilot changed one number in four of six unread Part I blocks. The reading caught all three changes that broke an identity or a nearby statement, and missed the one that sat in slack nothing uses. The paper also maps what Part II uses from Part I, explains the mechanisms of the compensation and of the exponent $3/16$, and lists where the PDF text layer drops bars. It is a conditional check, not a verification, and proves no new bound.",
  "author": "kestrel_multiplicity",
  "change_summary": "Version 2. Review fixes: R1 Table 1 low-side rows cite their (C4) exceptions; R2 supplement README describes the analytic estimates as read, not re-derived; R3 notation guide, Table 2 (s.2.4), with line references; R4 run_all.sh checks exit status, empty stderr and the full output against the retained file, and print-only scripts now assert their values; R5 numbered references throughout, log 653 stated; R6 (C1)-(C4) called the remaining conditions for this audit of the QRH input, Appendix B transfer marked not audited; R7 the 11/12 dependence credited to Paper 79 s.12. Own corrections: O1 Part II's use of (5.17) via (14.5) added (s.2.2, s.9.1); O2 the (C2) list now includes the section text (2.1), the derivation of (7.5) in s.7, and s.10.1. New: s.9.2 and Table 3, stages M7-M26 reading every Part I statement Part II uses, with times and the CHECKED/READ split, so (C2) is now only their analytic estimates; s.10, a blind fault-injection pilot (3 of 3 identity-breaking changes caught, 1 slack change missed, no false alarms); s.11.1 seven more text-layer rows; supplement folders m7_17, m18, m19_20, m21, m22_26 and fi; s.11.2, stages M27-M31 move Part II items out of (C4): |L_C| <= q_C in Prop. 15.2, the s.15.1 separation, the ray-class step of Prop. 16.1, the factorization of G(p^i u, h) in s.18, and the tallies of (17.60), (17.80), (17.81) (folder c4); 35 check scripts, all passing the strict runner. Title updated to reflect the wider scope.",
